    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3389/fclim.2026.1779183",
    "abstract": "This study addresses challenges in predicting extreme weather events, including intense rainfall, hail, and strong winds. Such events pose significant threats to infrastructure and human life, and their frequency has been increasing due to climate change. We show, that integrating climate forecasts with modern machine learning techniques improves prediction accuracy and helps identify regions where these events may become more frequent and dangerous. To achieve reliable predictions, we propose a robust neural network architecture that outperforms several common baselines in accuracy and reliability. Our model leverages problem-specific physics encapsulated in Coupled Model Intercomparison Project data. The analysis also highlight the impact of rugged terrain on the risk distribution of extreme weather events. The proposed model has significant potential to support mid-term adaptation strategies in response to climate change.",
    "field": "Machine learning for climate hazard prediction",
    "problem": "Existing methods struggle to predict long-term changes in severe convective storm frequency due to the scarcity of high-quality historical event data and the limited accuracy of climate model outputs for direct hazard assessment.",
    "method": "The authors propose a neural network framework using autoencoders on climate model data to estimate severe convective storm frequency, by first performing daily hazard classification and then aggregating results into monthly risk maps to evaluate the impact of climate change.",
    "task": "The task is to predict the changing frequency of severe convective storms under climate change, using a neural network to analyze climate model projections.",
    "model": [
        "MRI-CGCM3"
    ],
